What was derived, and where it lives now
----------------------------------------

Six effects and the small amount of substrate they need. These are rewritten
rather than copied: the layout is format-aware instead of hard-coded to
1080x1920, every colour is a theme token, text measurement no longer breaks
React's rules of hooks, and captions understand right-to-left script.

  src/lib/ease.ts              Easing names and curve conventions (see below)
  src/lib/core.ts              FxCtx / FxDef effect contract, face
                               interpolation, camera layer transform,
                               face-guard mask
  src/lib/layout.ts            Platform safe zones, chin and band placement
  src/effects/camera.ts        lean-in, slow-push
  src/effects/KineticCaption.tsx   kinetic-caption
  src/effects/NameTag.tsx      name-tag
  src/effects/BottomFade.tsx   bottom-fade
  src/effects/TextBehind.tsx   text-behind

The easing curves are OUR implementation, not GSAP's. The reference calls
gsap.parseEase for curves named 'expo.out', 'power2.in' and so on. GSAP ships
under the GreenSock "Standard 'no charge' License", not MIT, so it is not a
dependency here. src/lib/ease.ts implements the same named families from the
standard formulas and is endpoint-exact; motion will be very close to the
reference but is not claimed to be identical frame for frame.


What was deliberately NOT taken
-------------------------------

  - The Stage / Film renderer, the person matte, and the three.js and
    mediapipe passes. Those need a face track and an alpha matte that this
    engine does not produce yet.
  - Effects built on three.js (carousel-spin, orbit, prop-3d) and the chart,
    tier-list and glass-widget sets. They are creator-metrics furniture, not
    kajian material.
  - lib/fonts.ts. It loads all six of its faces with subsets: ['latin'],
    which makes Arabic fall back silently to a system face. See
    docs/arabic-shaping.md.


